  <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Tsampa (Zanba)</h1>
  <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-500">The original energy bar. Roasted highland barley flour mixed with butter tea into a dough-like paste. For centuries, this has been the staple that powered Tibetan nomads across the Himalayan plateau.</p>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">What Is Tsampa?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Tsampa (u7c8fu7c91, zanba) is ground <strong>roasted highland barley flour</strong> (qingke), the single most important food in Tibetan culture for over a thousand years. The barley is first washed, sun-dried, then roasted in a pan until the grains turn golden brown and release a nutty aroma. The roasted barley is then stone-ground into a fine, slightly coarse flour with a warm, toasted scent. This flour is tsampa &mdash; shelf-stable for months, highly portable, and requiring no cooking.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Tsampa is almost never eaten dry. The traditional way is to pour hot yak butter tea into a bowl of tsampa flour and use your fingers to knead it into a firm, dough-like consistency. The resulting ball is eaten piece by piece. Some Tibetans add sugar, dried yak cheese, or crushed nuts. It is dense, mildly nutty, and surprisingly filling &mdash; a handful can sustain a herder for an entire afternoon of work at 4,000 meters.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Nutritional Value</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Highland barley (qingke) is notably high in beta-glucan, a soluble fiber shown to lower cholesterol and regulate blood sugar. Tsampa is rich in complex carbohydrates, B vitamins, and minerals including iron and zinc. Combined with the fat from yak butter, one serving (about 100g) delivers roughly <strong>450&ndash;500 calories</strong> &mdash; perfect for high-altitude energy demands. It is naturally vegan aside from the butter tea mixing.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Where to Try Tsampa in Shangri-La</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 space-y-5">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Tibetan Tea Houses</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Any traditional Tibetan tea house in Dukezong Old Town serves tsampa alongside butter tea. The combined cost for a bowl of tsampa and a pot of butter tea is <strong>15&ndash;25 RMB</strong>. The tea house owners are often happy to demonstrate the proper kneading technique if you ask.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Guesthouse Breakfast</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Most Tibetan-style guesthouses include tsampa as part of their breakfast spread, usually alongside butter tea, boiled eggs, and steamed buns.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Modern Tsampa Shops</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">On the main street of Dukezong Old Town, a few shops sell tsampa-based desserts mixing the flour with yogurt, honey, and crushed walnuts. More palatable for first-timers at <strong>15&ndash;20 RMB</strong>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Where to Buy Tsampa</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong>Packaged tsampa flour:</strong> Sold at the morning market (Dukezong east gate), local grocery stores, and even at the Shangri-La bus station shop. <strong>15&ndash;30 RMB per 500g bag</strong>. The best quality is stone-ground with visible barley bran specks.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong>Specialty souvenir shops</strong> along Sifang Street sell beautifully packaged tsampa gift boxes (50&ndash;100 RMB) that include small bags of tsampa flour, dried yak cheese, and crushed walnuts &mdash; everything needed for a Tibetan breakfast. These make excellent gifts.</p>
  </section>

